From b618efdb06de145891c06abf714fb9f6f80f06eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: akapug <akapug@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 17:28:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/16] sycl: XMX small-batch mat-mul for Q4_K, Q6_K and
 IQ4_XS; from 4 columns

Extend the XMX mat-mul from Q5_K to Q4_K and Q6_K, read in their reorder layouts, and to
IQ4_XS, which has no reorder layout here and is read in its plain 136-byte blocks. The
dispatch now tries the XMX path before the reorder check, and each type states which layout
its kernel reads. Q4_K takes Q5_K's nibble path without the high bit. Q6_K's 6-bit codes use
the same fp16 trick (code | 0x6400 is 1024 + code), less 1056. IQ4_XS decodes each byte's two
codes through a 256-entry fp16 pair table in shared memory: per-weight register lookups left
the kernel ALU bound, 1.7x slower than dp4a. Its fields are taken from the loaded words by
shifts, because reading them through a block pointer into that array broke strict aliasing
and returned NaN.

At most 1280 row groups run at once and the sub-groups walk the rest: a 248320-row output
head launched whole ran 1.3x slower than dp4a. The path now starts at 4 columns: at 3 each
kernel alone still beats dp4a, but Qwen3-4B decoded 3% slower as a whole. IQ4_XS gate/up pairs
keep the fused dp4a kernel, which beats two XMX launches for them.

Arc Pro B70, test-backend-ops perf, dp4a -> XMX, us at 4 / 8 columns:
  Q4_K   5120x17408 194 / 389 -> 115 / 117    17408x5120 166 / 360 -> 126 / 127
  Q6_K   5120x17408 237 / 398 -> 151 / 153    17408x5120 215 / 360 -> 172 / 174
  Q6_K   248320x5120 (output head) 2882 / 4794 -> 2317 / 2343
  IQ4_XS 5120x17408 190 / 259 -> 175 / 177    (17408x5120 173 / 240 -> 180 / 183, not taken)
Qwen3-4B with Q5_K gate/up and Q6_K down, llama-batched-bench decode: 4 sequences +11%,
8 sequences +77%; 1-3 unchanged. test-backend-ops MUL_MAT and MUL_MAT_VEC_FUSION pass
against the CPU; the XMX eval cases cover all four types.
